      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals",
      "problem": "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{6}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{6}^{3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+5 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
